WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.893 --> 00:00:18.291
Hi, I’m John Green,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.291 --> 00:00:24.134
author, vlog brother, and whatever the&nbsp;
opposite of a mosquito enthusiast is.

00:00:24.134 --> 00:00:27.836
You never want to get up close&nbsp;
and personal with mosquitos…

00:00:27.836 --> 00:00:31.791
unless it’s like this, a&nbsp;
microscope’s distance away.

00:00:32.848 --> 00:00:36.874
Under the lens, the mosquito&nbsp;
undergoes a transformation of sorts.

00:00:36.874 --> 00:00:42.480
Before, it was a pesky, biting insect whose&nbsp;
presence was felt at best through the itchy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:48.206
bumps left on our skin, and at worst&nbsp;
through a whole slew of diseases.

00:00:48.206 --> 00:00:53.878
But under the microscope, mosquitos&nbsp;
undergo a metamorphosis sculpted in gold.

00:00:53.878 --> 00:00:57.069
The buzzing body takes on a life of its own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.069 --> 00:01:02.802
its usual role as menace lying far&nbsp;
beyond the margins of the screen.

00:01:02.802 --> 00:01:05.545
The mosquito is beautiful,

00:01:05.545 --> 00:01:10.916
a sentence I never expected to say, and&nbsp;
that I assume you never expected to hear.

00:01:13.468 --> 00:01:19.197
Even the parts that are less elegant&nbsp;
become charming, like their legs.

00:01:19.197 --> 00:01:24.711
They may look like a Christmas tree that’s&nbsp;
collapsed into a pile of Cheeto dust right now.

00:01:24.711 --> 00:01:30.480
But when you think about the tiny hooks along&nbsp;
their surface that allow the mosquito to latch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:37.083
onto surfaces, and of the scales that help&nbsp;
repel water so they can walk across ponds…

00:01:37.083 --> 00:01:44.222
well, then you start to realize you’re looking at&nbsp;
an incredible feat of, you know, feet.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.086
And of course, there are the wings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.086 --> 00:01:51.000
which are always an impressive part&nbsp;
of nature to witness in action.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:58.155
Along the edges, you can see scaly hairs that&nbsp;
the insect uses to process its surroundings.

00:01:58.155 --> 00:02:00.951
Mosquito wings are unique in several ways.

00:02:00.951 --> 00:02:06.180
They’re unusually long and narrow, and&nbsp;
they flap at an incredibly high rate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:09.630
even compared to other insects of their size,

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:13.215
around 800 times per second.

00:02:13.215 --> 00:02:18.240
To learn how those wings work, scientists&nbsp;
needed to use high-speed cameras capturing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:25.263
10,000 frames per second so they could&nbsp;
distinguish each step of that rapid movement.

00:02:25.263 --> 00:02:28.537
And in the process of flying, the movement of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.537 --> 00:02:33.832
wings creates the buzzing that&nbsp;
warns us a mosquito is nearby.

00:02:33.832 --> 00:02:40.267
It’s an infuriating noise when it’s right against&nbsp;
your ear. But for the mosquitos, it’s a signal,

00:02:40.267 --> 00:02:43.503
an instrument that guides them towards each other.

00:02:43.503 --> 00:02:48.840
Male and female mosquitos will even&nbsp;
go so far as to converge on the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:52.162
wing-flapping frequency when they are courting.

00:02:54.568 --> 00:02:59.280
And with that image in mind, we’ve conjured&nbsp;
up a vision of the mosquito that is both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:04.666
beautiful and romantic, flirting&nbsp;
wistfully through wing flap patterns.

00:03:04.666 --> 00:03:08.909
All it takes to shatter that notion is this part,

00:03:08.909 --> 00:03:10.513
the proboscis.

00:03:10.513 --> 00:03:14.460
This is what allows the mosquito to drink from us.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:17.915
At first, the proboscis looks&nbsp;
like a simple instrument,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.915 --> 00:03:21.957
like a very poke-y straw that&nbsp;
connects with our blood vessels.

00:03:21.957 --> 00:03:30.360
But in reality, it is a complex machine, made up&nbsp;
of six thin spear-like structures called stylets.

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:36.985
Two stylets hold the tissue apart while another&nbsp;
pair act like drill bits digging through our skin.

00:03:36.985 --> 00:03:40.860
And then a stylet called the 
labrum sniffs out blood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:46.459
vessels using receptors that turn&nbsp;
our body’s chemistry into a map.

00:03:46.459 --> 00:03:52.279
And when it does find the vessel, the&nbsp;
labrum converts itself into a pipeline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.279 --> 00:03:55.236
carrying blood to the mosquito.

00:03:55.236 --> 00:03:58.500
The sixth and last stylet helps the labrum with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:03.433
its task by injecting mosquito&nbsp;
saliva into our blood vessels.

00:04:03.433 --> 00:04:06.771
This is not just gross mosquito backwash.

00:04:06.771 --> 00:04:10.020
Mosquito saliva contains enzymes that dilate our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:14.238
blood vessels and prevent our&nbsp;
immune system from responding.

00:04:16.108 --> 00:04:19.491
At least, that’s what the female mosquito does.

00:04:19.491 --> 00:04:26.026
Because all of this work, it’s not actually&nbsp;
to provide the mosquito’s main meal.

00:04:26.026 --> 00:04:31.330
Mosquitos sustain themselves&nbsp;
on nectar and fruit juices.

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:38.292
Blood, on the other hand, is about the next&nbsp;
generation, and the eggs that will make them.

00:04:39.977 --> 00:04:42.787
So male mosquitos don’t consume blood.

00:04:42.787 --> 00:04:46.809
Their proboscis isn’t even&nbsp;
strong enough to pierce our skin.

00:04:46.809 --> 00:04:51.656
But female mosquitos need blood for&nbsp;
reproduction, using the nutrients—

00:04:51.656 --> 00:04:53.332
particularly iron—

00:04:53.332 --> 00:04:55.661
to fuel the growth of their eggs.

00:04:55.661 --> 00:05:01.646
Over her lifetime, the female&nbsp;
mosquito may lay around 500 eggs.

00:05:01.646 --> 00:05:08.923
So if the physical discomfort that follows a bite&nbsp;
is not enough misery for you, rest uneasily in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.923 --> 00:05:15.787
knowledge that your blood has fueled the birth of&nbsp;
the next round of mosquitos that will plague you,

00:05:15.787 --> 00:05:17.749
and the world.

00:05:17.749 --> 00:05:22.704
And “plague” is the obvious word to&nbsp;
go with when it comes to mosquitos.

00:05:22.704 --> 00:05:26.580
The Centers for Disease Control&nbsp;
and Prevention call mosquitos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:33.000
“the World’s Deadliest Animal” because of the&nbsp;
essential role they play in spreading disease.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:38.040
A shortlist of mosquito’s notorious&nbsp;
diseases includes malaria, dengue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:42.771
West Nile, Zika, and lymphatic filariasis.

00:05:42.771 --> 00:05:50.098
The actual organisms responsible for these&nbsp;
diseases include protists, worms, and viruses.

00:05:50.098 --> 00:05:57.487
But their spread is driven by mosquitos, hopping&nbsp;
from person to person to get their blood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.487 --> 00:06:03.418
and leaving behind an unwanted memento&nbsp;
of the microcosmos when they leave.

00:06:03.418 --> 00:06:06.960
Malaria, for example, is the potentially fatal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:12.368
work of parasitic protists that&nbsp;
belong to the genus Plasmodium.

00:06:12.368 --> 00:06:17.919
When an infected mosquito bites a human,&nbsp;
the plasmodium quickly travels to the liver,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.919 --> 00:06:21.378
usually reaching its destination in 20 minutes.

00:06:21.378 --> 00:06:24.548
And then the parasite reproduces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.548 --> 00:06:30.472
moving through different stages of their&nbsp;
life cycle as they infect red blood cells.

00:06:30.472 --> 00:06:34.680
When another mosquito comes along&nbsp;
for blood, it will end up taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:41.201
some of the parasite with it, keeping
the cycle going and going and going.

00:06:41.201 --> 00:06:51.360
In 2021, there were 247 million cases&nbsp;
of malaria worldwide, and 619,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:55.084
people died from the disease.

00:06:55.084 --> 00:06:59.339
To try and prevent these deaths,&nbsp;
humans have tried many strategies to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.339 --> 00:07:07.355
curb the mosquito population or their ability to&nbsp;
spread disease. Insecticides, genetic engineering…

00:07:07.355 --> 00:07:10.154
there have been a wide range of tools.

00:07:10.154 --> 00:07:15.756
But we want to end with the one that&nbsp;
resonates most with the microcosmos.

00:07:15.756 --> 00:07:22.933
It would look kind of like this, an array of&nbsp;
ciliates borne across the skin of a larva.

00:07:22.933 --> 00:07:25.320
When scientists have pitted the vorticella—

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:27.826
a Journey to the Microcosmos favorite—

00:07:27.826 --> 00:07:34.612
against the larvae of certain mosquito&nbsp;
species, the mosquitos have fared quite poorly.

00:07:34.612 --> 00:07:40.055
It’s not clear why or how,&nbsp;
but the mosquito larvae die.

00:07:40.055 --> 00:07:44.178
The vorticella might prevent them&nbsp;
from staying on the surface of water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.178 --> 00:07:46.524
getting in the way of their respiration.

00:07:46.524 --> 00:07:52.978
Or the vorticella might simply be poisoning the&nbsp;
mosquitos with chemical secretions of their own.

00:07:52.978 --> 00:07:59.305
And if only the mosquito weren’t a&nbsp;
mosquito, perhaps we could sympathize.

00:07:59.305 --> 00:08:02.760
Because we know the plight of&nbsp;
a pest attached to our skin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:06.755
leaving behind misfortunes both small and deadly.

00:08:06.755 --> 00:08:14.561
But the microcosmos is the beginning and end of&nbsp;
so many journeys, and even the mighty mosquito—

00:08:14.561 --> 00:08:17.457
the vector of so many diseases—

00:08:17.457 --> 00:08:20.781
can find its own wanderings cut short .

00:08:24.552 --> 00:08:31.656
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:08:31.656 --> 00:08:33.960
We also want to say thank&nbsp;
you to all of those people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:37.001
whose names you’re seeing on the screen right now.

00:08:37.001 --> 00:08:42.963
Those are some of our Patreon patrons and we&nbsp;
could not continue to make this show without them.

00:08:42.963 --> 00:08:46.704
If you’d like to see more from our&nbsp;
Master of Microscopes, James Weiss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.704 --> 00:08:49.804
you can check out Jam and Germs on Instagram.

00:08:49.804 --> 00:08:55.649
And if you’d like to see more from us, there&nbsp;
should be a subscribe button somewhere nearby.

